---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Open Tech Era"
  description="A perspective on the rapid evolution of technology and a future where technical capability belongs to everyone."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-6 py-16 sm:py-24">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent">Field notes · 01</p>
      <h1 class="mt-5 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-fg sm:text-6xl">
        The Open Tech Era
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        Technology is moving from something we use into something we shape. The next chapter belongs
        to people who can make it useful, understandable, and human.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3 font-mono text-xs text-fg-muted">
        <span class="rounded-full border border-line-strong bg-surface px-3 py-1">Technology</span>
        <span class="rounded-full border border-line-strong bg-surface px-3 py-1">
          The future of work
        </span>
        <span>September 2026 · 6 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="mt-16 grid gap-12 border-t border-line-strong pt-12 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_0.38fr]">
      <div class="prose max-w-none">
        <p class="text-2xl leading-relaxed text-fg">
          The pace of change can feel impossible to follow. Every week brings a new model, platform,
          device, or way to automate a task that used to require a team. But beneath the noise is a
          more important shift: technology is becoming more accessible to more people.
        </p>

        <h2>From specialists to participants</h2>
        <p>
          For a long time, the digital world was divided into builders and users. Infrastructure
          lived behind specialist doors, software required expensive tooling, and technical language
          created a barrier between an idea and its implementation. That boundary is dissolving.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cloud platforms, open source, low-code tools, and natural-language interfaces are giving
          more people the ability to test an idea, launch a service, or solve a problem for their
          community. The best outcome is not that everyone becomes a software engineer. It is that
          everyone gets more agency over the tools that shape their work and lives.
        </p>

        <h2>A future where capability is shared</h2>
        <p>
          I expect the most valuable technical skill of the next decade to be translation: turning a
          human goal into a reliable system, then explaining that system clearly enough for others
          to improve it. Technical fluency will become as common as financial or media literacy is
          today.
        </p>
        <p>
          In that future, a teacher can build a learning tool without waiting for a large product
          team. A small business can automate its operations without surrendering its data. A public
          service can use intelligent systems while keeping accountability in the hands of people.
          Technology becomes less of a gate and more of a shared language.
        </p>

        <h2>What still matters</h2>
        <p>
          Faster tools do not remove the need for judgment. Security, accessibility, privacy,
          resilience, and thoughtful design matter more when more people can create and deploy
          systems. The role of a technical consultant is increasingly to make that power practical:
          create a sound foundation, reduce unnecessary complexity, and help people make confident
          decisions.
        </p>
        <p>
          The future will not be defined by technology alone. It will be defined by who gets to
          participate in it, who understands its tradeoffs, and whether we build systems that expand
          human possibility instead of narrowing it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <aside class="self-start border-l border-accent/30 pl-6">
        <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-accent">The thesis</p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-fg">
          Technical fluency is becoming a shared civic skill, not a private advantage.
        </p>
        <a href="/book" class="mt-8 inline-block text-sm font-semibold text-accent hover:text-fg">
          Talk through the future &rarr;
        </a>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
